Prod-ready” jstack checklist for a hung JVM (deadlock vs starvation vs IO)
============================================
Step 1 — Confirm “is the JVM actually stuck?”
============================================
* Request symptoms: latency exploding, throughput near 0, errors/timeouts rising
* Thread pool symptoms: all worker threads busy/blocked, queue growing
* CPU clue:
    * CPU low/idle + requests hung → often deadlock / lock wait / IO wait
    * CPU high → might be hot loop or GC pressure (different path)
(You can do the CPU check via top/htop in Linux or container metrics in k8s/OpenShift.)

============================================
Step 2 — Take 3 thread dumps (time dimension)
============================================

This is the core move.

    jstack <pid> > td1.txt
    sleep 10
    jstack <pid> > td2.txt
    sleep 10
    jstack <pid> > td3.txt

============================================
Step 3 — First quick scan (the “big 3”)
============================================
A) Look for explicit monitor deadlock
Search in each dump for:
* Found one Java-level deadlock:
If present → done. You have a confirmed monitor deadlock from one dump.

============================================
B) Count threads by state
============================================
Skim for many threads in:
    BLOCKED → monitor contention / possible deadlock
    WAITING (parking) → AQS locks, latches, thread pools
    RUNNABLE but doing native IO → can still be “stuck” (socket read)

============================================
C) Find the request threads / worker threads
============================================
Look for names like:
    http-nio-8080-exec-* (Tomcat)
    reactor-http-nio-* (Netty/Reactor)
    your custom executors (pool-*, ForkJoinPool, etc.)

============================================
Step 4 — Decide which bucket you’re in
============================================

Bucket 1: Monitor deadlock (synchronized) ✅
--------
Signature:
    * BLOCKED (on object monitor)
    * lines like:
    - waiting to lock <0x...>
    - locked <0x...>
often with the explicit deadlock banner
Conclusion: deadlock (even from one dump if banner is there).

Bucket 2: AQS lock deadlock or permanent lock wait (ReentrantLock / RWLock) ⚠️
---------
Signature in dumps:
lots of threads show:
    * WAITING (parking)
    * Unsafe.park
    * LockSupport.park
    * AbstractQueuedSynchronizer.acquire
    * ReentrantLock.lock / ReadWriteLock.writeLock().lock etc.
    * then your app frame com.yourapp...:line

How to conclude:
    * compare td1 vs td2 vs td3
    * If the same threads are parked at the same lock call sites (same line numbers) across dumps → no progress.
    * Then determine which of these two sub-causes:
    2A) Circular wait (classic deadlock)
        Two or more threads each stuck trying to acquire a “second lock”
        Often each thread’s top app frame is a lock() call, in different places
        Pattern persists across dumps
    2B) Lock never released (bug)
        Many threads waiting for the same lock acquisition line
        But you cannot find evidence of anyone making progress / unlocking
        Often caused by missing unlock() in a finally, or exception path

Pragmatic conclusion in prod:
    The JVM is stuck on AQS lock acquisition with no progress across time; treat as deadlock/permanent lock wait.

Bucket 3: Thread pool starvation / self-deadlock (very common!)
------
This one is sneaky and looks like deadlock.
Signature:
    Many threads are waiting on futures:
    CompletableFuture.join/get
    FutureTask.get
    CountDownLatch.await

* Those futures depend on tasks scheduled onto the same exhausted pool
    Example smell:
        All pool-1-thread-* are blocked waiting for work that must run on pool-1-*
How to conclude:
    In dumps, pool threads are blocked on waits, and there are no free threads to run queued tasks.
    Often the same across multiple dumps.
    Fix (later) is: separate pools, avoid blocking joins inside pool threads, use async composition, etc.

Bucket 4: IO hang / downstream dependency
------
Signature:
    * Threads in:
       java.net.SocketInputStream.socketRead0
       HTTP client calls (Apache, OkHttp, Netty)
       JDBC driver reads
       Many request threads blocked waiting on remote calls

Conclusion:
Not a JVM deadlock; it’s an external dependency or missing timeout.

Next step:
    verify client timeouts
    check downstream health
    look for connection pool exhaustion
=================================
Step 5 — The “grep” moves that save time
=================================
When you have td1.txt / td2.txt / td3.txt, search for:

AQS waits:
    AbstractQueuedSynchronizer
    LockSupport.park
    Unsafe.park
    ReentrantLock
    ReentrantReadWriteLock

Blocking waits / starvation:
    CompletableFuture.get
    CompletableFuture.join
    FutureTask.get
    CountDownLatch.await
    ForkJoinPool

IO waits:
    socketRead0
    SSLSocket
    HttpClient
    okhttp
    JDBC classes

Your code:
    com.yourcompany. (whatever your package root is)

